        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "You can use these six principles to prepare yourself to read a difficult document. First, highlight the question in the problem statement and the main claim that answers it (see pp. 88–92). Next, for each section, highlight its introduction, point, and key concepts. If you don't find them in the introduction to a section, look for them at the end of the section. Finally, read through just the parts that you highlighted. When you then begin reading in detail, you will have in mind an overview that will help you better understand and remember the rest.",
            "block_bbox": [
                126,
                440,
                785,
                608
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 7,
            "global_block_id": 7,
            "global_group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    126.0,
                    440.0
                ],
                [
                    785.0,
                    440.0
                ],
                [
                    785.0,
                    608.0
                ],
                [
                    126.0,
                    608.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "In the limited space we have here, we can't illustrate these principles with entire documents or even long sections. So we'll use paragraphs and ask you to relate their structure to that of a whole section of a document.",
            "block_bbox": [
                91,
                664,
                783,
                739
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 8,
            "global_block_id": 8,
            "global_group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
